00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:44.480
Roberts before getting married, and Sidney’s&nbsp;
aunt is Kate Roberts. She is the realtor that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:48.080
this sign is referring to. Earlier drafts of&nbsp;
the script make mention of her profession.

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:51.440
Kate takes Sidney in while she’s back in&nbsp;
Woodsboro. We can assume that Sidney’s dad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:55.200
moved away or died, because he’s not in the&nbsp;
movie. So Sidney is staying with a realtor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:57.840
who happens to be selling the&nbsp;
location of “Stab-a-thon”.

00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:01.680
And this is another similarity she has to Laurie&nbsp;
Strode, who, after losing her parents is taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:05.520
in by the Strode family, and her new adoptive&nbsp;
father is a realtor who is selling a house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:09.120
the Myers House, which was the location&nbsp;
of more of a literal Stab-a-thon.

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:12.560
With Sidney being part of the older generation&nbsp;
now, she takes responsibility for the kids’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:16.400
safety. Towards the end, she instructs Jill to&nbsp;
hide under a bed and then makes it look as if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:20.800
she’s helped her escape off the balcony. This is&nbsp;
very similar to Laurie’s strategy in Halloween.

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:22.720
Of course, there’s one line where Sid is made out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:25.280
to look more less like Laurie&nbsp;
and more like her brother…

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:30.560
– You just won’t die will you? Who&nbsp;
are you? Michael f*cking Myers?

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:33.360
– The last Halloween reference I want&nbsp;
to bring up is this poster seen in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:37.120
the classroom where Cinema Club is held.&nbsp;
If you’ve seen Rob Zombie’s Halloween 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:42.080
you know it is not a remake of Halloween 2. Rob&nbsp;
Zombie’s Halloween 1 is a remake of the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:47.440
Halloween, however Rob Zombie’s Halloween&nbsp;
2 goes off in it’s own… strange direction.

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:51.040
I’m guessing this was likely a hint about what&nbsp;
the plans were for the Scream franchise. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:55.520
January 2010, screenwriter Kevin Williamson,&nbsp;
who had returned after an absence in Scream 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.520 --> 00:12:59.200
stated that he was contracted to&nbsp;
make Scream 4 and Scream 5. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:03.280
the fourth movie underperformed at the box&nbsp;
office, director Wes Craven got brain cancer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:07.600
and the fifth installment had to be put on&nbsp;
the shelf. Save that one for a rainy decade.

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:09.920
But as long as we’re talking&nbsp;
about the meta of Scre4m,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:14.438
I’ve also gotta mention what is perhaps&nbsp;
the most meta thing in the whole franchise.

00:13:19.284 --> 00:13:23.437
(melancholy music)

00:13:23.437 --> 00:13:27.600
Remember that cop character, Anthony Perkins?&nbsp;
Well not only is he named after a Psycho actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:30.400
but he also appeared in Scary Movie 3. Scary Movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:33.680
is a parody of Scream and Scream&nbsp;
is a satire of the horror genre.

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:36.960
If you’ve kept up with my Scream Things You&nbsp;
Missed series, you already know that the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:41.040
Scream contained many references to slasher&nbsp;
movies, including Wes Craven’s own work. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:45.440
Scream 4 focuses on movies that were remade.&nbsp;
The new version of the video store scene is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:49.520
the Cinema Club scene where we see a whole bunch&nbsp;
of movie posters, like this one: The Hills Have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:54.800
Eyes. This checks both boxes, it was directed&nbsp;
by Wes Craven and it received a remake in 2006.

00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:58.796
If you’re really paying attention&nbsp;
you’ll also notice the poster for The People Under The Stairs,

00:13:58.796 --> 00:14:01.506
a 1991 Wes&nbsp;
Craven production. Before his death,

00:14:01.506 --> 00:14:04.320
Craven planned to remake this,&nbsp;
but never quite got around to it.

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:08.400
There’s also a poster for George Romero’s&nbsp;
Dawn of the Dead. It was remade in 2004.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:12.560
The Thing is a remake of The Thing From&nbsp;
Another World from 1951. You may notice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:16.720
the poster on the left is not a horror movie,&nbsp;
it’s a film called Zack and Miri Make a Porno.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:20.240
This is actually a continuation of a series&nbsp;
of easter eggs between Wes Craven and Kevin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:24.000
Smith. I talked about this in greater detail&nbsp;
in my Scream 3 episode, but the two directors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:27.520
have been exchanging cameos and easter eggs&nbsp;
in their movies since the original Scream.

00:14:27.520 --> 00:14:29.920
If you look around the classroom,&nbsp;
you’ll also see that the Cinema&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.920 --> 00:14:33.600
Club are big fans of the Stab franchise.&nbsp;
You can see the Stab poster right here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:37.680
and the Stab 3 poster back in the corner. An&nbsp;
interesting note here is the tagline for Stab&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:42.560
3 is “Hollywood Horror”. Which means this is not&nbsp;
the same movie we saw being shot in Scream 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:46.480
whose tagline was “Return To Woodsboro.” It&nbsp;
seems that since all the cast was killed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:50.480
the studio decided to make Stab 3 about&nbsp;
that, rather than trying to recast for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:54.800
Return To Woodsboro. Little did they know, the&nbsp;
story would return to Woodsboro all on its own.

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:58.720
Behind this shelf is the Stab 5 poster,&nbsp;
and the Stab 6 poster. But that’s really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:02.800
only just the beginning of the display of&nbsp;
their Stab fandom. We see much more that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:06.400
night at Stab-a-thon where there are a lot&nbsp;
of background details you may have missed.

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:10.400
Here’s a girl dressed as Casey Becker, posing&nbsp;
with a decoration of the “gutted” Casey Becker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.400 --> 00:15:13.840
which you can see more of behind the crowd&nbsp;
right here. Another display shows Tatum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:17.840
Riley hanging out of the garage flap. Many of&nbsp;
the attendees are wearing costumes of Scream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:21.680
and Stab characters. Something that is very&nbsp;
accurate if you’ve ever been to a screening.

00:15:21.680 --> 00:15:24.960
These two guys in the audience are both&nbsp;
dressed as Dewey, and one of them even has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:28.240
a mustache hat. There’s another Casey&nbsp;
Becker on the left. A lot of the kids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:32.640
have Woodsboro High letterman jackets, like&nbsp;
Casey’s boyfriend Steve Orth, but then again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:36.640
those are also their actual high school jackets,&nbsp;
so I won’t count that one. But I will count this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:40.720
couple dressed as Gale and Dewey, Gale in her&nbsp;
green news dress and Dewey in his uniform.

00:15:40.720 --> 00:15:46.146
– [Gale] This party is all about the Stab&nbsp;
movies, come on! How meta can you get?&nbsp;

00:15:46.146 --> 00:15:49.040
– [Dewey] How what-a can you get?
– [Gale] I don’t know, I heard him say it.`

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:52.160
– Gale places cameras around the party much&nbsp;
like she did in Scream, so that she can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.160 --> 00:15:55.600
watch from her vehicle. In fact, one of the&nbsp;
cameras is right beneath the movie screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.600 --> 00:15:58.800
just as the surveillance camera in&nbsp;
Scream was placed under Stu’s television.

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:02.480
Now you’re probably wondering: are there&nbsp;
differences between the version of Stab we saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:08.000
in Scream 2 and what we see in Scream 4? So I cut&nbsp;
both films apart and lined them both up, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:12.560
answer is: yes there are a lot of differences.&nbsp;
Enough to make a whole video about it, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:16.480
I plan on doing. But for this video, I’m only&nbsp;
gonna talk about the ones that are interesting.

00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:19.920
In Scream 4 we see this card at the&nbsp;
beginning: A Robert Rodriguez Film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:22.240
This sort-of qualifies as a&nbsp;
crew reference easter egg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:25.680
because Robert Rodriguez was brought in&nbsp;
to film the Stab footage for Scream 2.

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:29.360
After lining up all the cut points, there’s an&nbsp;
interesting synchronization that occurs. Which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:34.240
is that Gale gets stabbed at the same time as&nbsp;
Casey in Stab, which in Scream 2, occurred at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:37.840
same time Maureen was stabbed inside the movie&nbsp;
theater. Just thought that was kind of cool.

00:16:37.840 --> 00:16:41.280
Before being attacked, Gale holds her&nbsp;
camera by her waist, looking behind her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:43.360
unintentionally recreating the moment from Scream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:46.400
where the characters in the news van&nbsp;
see Ghostface sneaking up behind Randy.

00:16:46.400 --> 00:16:48.000
– Behind you kid!
– Randy!

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:49.920
– This time it’s Dewey, concerned about Gale.

00:16:49.920 --> 00:16:52.400
– Gale! Gale behind you!

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:56.320
– This is more than just a nostalgic callback&nbsp;
though, it also sets up a scene later where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.320 --> 00:17:00.240
Robbie puts his headset on backwards. We’re&nbsp;
expecting Ghostface to appear behind him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:03.600
so it’s more of a surprise when the killer&nbsp;
ends up being right in front of him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:06.800
just another example of this movie’s&nbsp;
mission to do the unexpected.

00:17:06.800 --> 00:17:09.040
That also includes the film’s ending. We are led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:11.920
to believe that it will end at the&nbsp;
party, just like the original, but…

00:17:11.920 --> 00:17:13.600
– This is what the reboots do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.600 --> 00:17:16.800
they one-up the original ending.
– The Woodsboro Murders ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:20.960
at a party. So in the remake…
– The party’s the false ending. New rules.

00:17:20.960 --> 00:17:24.320
– However, this also ends up being a false&nbsp;
ending, the true ending takes place at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.320 --> 00:17:28.320
the hospital. Just as there were two false&nbsp;
openings, there are also two false endings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.320 --> 00:17:30.880
This is also something that happens in&nbsp;
the Halloween remake. The original ends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.880 --> 00:17:33.840
at the Doyle house, but the remake&nbsp;
continues on and ends at the Myers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:37.360
house. There are probably other examples&nbsp;
of remakes in the 2000s doing this though.

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:41.840
So in this case, we have the quote unquote&nbsp;
remake continuing on past the original. Whereas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:45.840
in the 90s, if you wanted to continue past&nbsp;
the original, you had to watch Scream 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:49.120
which contains a scene of a character&nbsp;
named Cici watching the movie Nosferatu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.120 --> 00:17:52.640
and getting killed shortly after. For this&nbsp;
reason, I find it fitting that Kirby’s room&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.640 --> 00:17:56.720
displays a Nosferatu poster, but who knows if&nbsp;
there was any actual meaning behind this choice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.720 --> 00:17:58.960
it’s kind of a stretch on my&nbsp;
part to make that connection.

00:17:58.960 --> 00:18:02.800
However, I am convinced that the wardrobe&nbsp;
choices were definitely done for a reason.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.800 --> 00:18:06.160
I talked before about how each character&nbsp;
in Scre4m represents one of the characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:10.640
from Scream. However, after the reveal of the&nbsp;
killers, all of those roles are reassigned.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.640 --> 00:18:14.480
Jill is now Billy, the mastermind behind&nbsp;
the murders. Charlie is still Stu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:17.600
the accomplice hoping to vent his&nbsp;
romantic frustrations with a knife.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:21.360
Trevor is now Neil Prescott, the tied&nbsp;
up victim who is going to be framed.

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:25.120
So it’s no mistake that Jill is wearing a&nbsp;
blue plaid shirt, just like what Billy wore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.120 --> 00:18:30.160
at the party, and Trevor is wearing this exact&nbsp;
outfit that Neil was captured in back in 1996.

00:18:30.160 --> 00:18:34.320
Other callbacks include Sidney escaping off the&nbsp;
roof, Charlie being tied to a chair like Steve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:38.960
Orth, Kirby being quizzed on horror movies like&nbsp;
Casey Becker, and of course, this iconic line.

00:18:38.960 --> 00:18:41.653
– Hello Sidney. Surprised?&nbsp;

00:18:41.653 --> 00:18:45.175
– Surprise, Sidney.
– Surprise, Sidney.

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:48.640
– I imagine she’s getting tired of&nbsp;
hearing that. In my personal opinion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:51.280
Scre4m was one of the more&nbsp;
satisfying surprises though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.280 --> 00:18:55.407
which makes all the clues about the&nbsp;
killers’ identities all the more impressive.

00:19:00.335 --> 00:19:04.000
(upbeat music)

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:07.600
Upon rewatching the movie, I noticed that&nbsp;
while Jill and Charlie pretend they aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.600 --> 00:19:11.440
particularly close, they do sit next to each&nbsp;
other in class, and Jill is the only one not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.440 --> 00:19:14.480
to take out her phone when everyone is&nbsp;
getting alerts about Jenny and Marnie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.480 --> 00:19:17.360
though I’m not sure why she doesn’t&nbsp;
at least pretend to be interested.

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:21.440
On a somewhat related note, fellow Zac&nbsp;
and fellow horror creator Zack Cherry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.440 --> 00:19:24.960
pointed out in his video that Sidney doesn’t&nbsp;
appear to have a cell phone in this movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:28.960
She receives calls on Jill’s cell and on&nbsp;
the Roberts’ landline, and after arriving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.960 --> 00:19:32.960
at Kirby’s she borrows Jill’s phone to place&nbsp;
an outgoing call to Dewey to plea for help.

00:19:32.960 --> 00:19:36.080
This is probably because Sidney is tired&nbsp;
of all the prank calls and doesn’t want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.080 --> 00:19:39.920
give any future copycat killers a means&nbsp;
of contacting her, it’s a nice touch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.920 --> 00:19:44.000
Mr. Cherry also attributes a lot of the kills&nbsp;
to Charlie, which many were surprised by,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:47.040
but I personally think it kind of makes&nbsp;
sense. Jill would have wanted to set it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.040 --> 00:19:50.160
up that way because of the fact that they’re&nbsp;
recording all of the kills and the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:53.840
she’s secretly planning to frame Charlie&nbsp;
as the one of the killers alongside Trevor.

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:56.720
It stands to reason that the police&nbsp;
would probably study these videos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.720 --> 00:19:59.280
and take note of the killer’s&nbsp;
height and broad shoulders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.280 --> 00:20:02.560
Charlie might also be the most forceful&nbsp;
Ghostface we’ve seen. Check this out.

00:20:02.774 --> 00:20:04.475
(woman wimpering)

00:20:04.640 --> 00:20:08.800
When I analyzed Scream 1, I mentioned that the&nbsp;
poster in Stu’s room matched a poster displayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.800 --> 00:20:12.960
in the video store, and that this linked his&nbsp;
character to the movie obsessed killer. A similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.960 --> 00:20:18.320
tactic is used here. Jill’s room is filled with&nbsp;
clues. This Farewell Republic poster and this…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:22.640
uhh, I don’t know, reverse Griffon?... are&nbsp;
both posters seen hanging in the Cinema Club.

00:20:22.640 --> 00:20:26.160
I will mention the possibility that there&nbsp;
is actually no meaning behind that. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:30.000
could have just reused posters and hoped&nbsp;
nobody would notice. Because this War of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:33.840
The Worlds poster in Jill’s room also shows&nbsp;
up in her English class. I can’t think of any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.840 --> 00:20:37.600
reason they’d want to link Jill to her English&nbsp;
classroom. So it’s very possible that the art&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:41.280
department just ran out of posters and I’m&nbsp;
just looking for things that aren’t there.

00:20:41.280 --> 00:20:45.040
But at the very least, we can conclude that&nbsp;
Jill is more interested in horror movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.040 --> 00:20:48.960
and serial killers than she likes to let on,&nbsp;
from her American Werewolf in London poster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.960 --> 00:20:54.640
to her Edgar Allan Poe-ster, to her Gale Weathers&nbsp;
books, books on psychology and serial killers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.640 --> 00:20:59.120
even her Fall Out Boy poster; this particular one&nbsp;
is the cover for their single I Don’t Care, whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.120 --> 00:21:04.400
chorus matches her MO for the movie. “I. Don’t.&nbsp;
Care what you think. As long as it’s about me.”

00:21:04.400 --> 00:21:07.840
This might be more happenstance, but this&nbsp;
is also off their album Folie a Deux,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.840 --> 00:21:11.280
which is French for madness in pairs, a&nbsp;
term that could probably be used to describe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.280 --> 00:21:15.600
each pair of Ghostface killers. I love this&nbsp;
album, and I’d love to talk more about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.600 --> 00:21:19.200
but this is not a music analysis&nbsp;
channel, so let’s get back to Scre4m.

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:22.640
After Olivia is killed, Charlie comes out&nbsp;
of the dark to attack Sidney and Jill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:26.720
and Jill receives a cut on her forearm,&nbsp;
the same spot Derek got cut in Scream 2.

00:21:26.720 --> 00:21:29.360
– No struggle? He just cut you and ran away?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:29.360 --> 00:21:30.813
You’re lucky he didn’t kill you.

00:21:30.813 --> 00:21:32.575
– Yeah, it’s awfully convenient.

00:21:32.575 --> 00:21:33.611
– Say what?

00:21:33.611 --> 00:21:34.495
– Nothing,

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:37.684
it’s just a shame he got away so easily.

00:21:37.684 --> 00:21:39.360
– It’s just a shame you got there too late!

00:21:39.360 --> 00:21:41.440
– Of course, Derek ended up not being guilty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.440 --> 00:21:45.120
so Dewey would probably want to avoid&nbsp;
making any similar accusation about Jill.

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:46.880
When Sidney speaks to Jill the following night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.880 --> 00:21:49.440
she has what she thinks is a&nbsp;
bonding moment with her cousin.

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:53.520
– [Sidney] You know when people say, ‘I&nbsp;
know how you feel’, but they’re really just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.520 --> 00:21:59.440
saying that because they have no freaking&nbsp;
idea how you feel? I know how you feel.

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:01.440
– I love that line, because in reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.440 --> 00:22:05.440
Sidney has no idea how Jill feels because&nbsp;
she doesn’t suspect her of being the killer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:05.440 --> 00:22:09.520
It’s like the sticker on the door of the&nbsp;
Cinema Club says: Not your usual suspects.

00:22:09.520 --> 00:22:13.200
Jill claims she could never handle the attention&nbsp;
of being the surviving victim, when in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:18.160
that’s exactly what she wants, even if her&nbsp;
15 minutes of fame really is only 15 minutes.

00:22:18.160 --> 00:22:21.680
And finally, at Kirby’s afterparty,&nbsp;
Trevor shows up unexpectedly, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.680 --> 00:22:23.920
just walks right in because the door is unlocked.

00:22:23.920 --> 00:22:27.280
– [Trevor] By the way Kirby, with everything&nbsp;
that’s going on right now, you probably shouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:29.120
leave that unlocked.
– [Charlie] Sorry.

00:22:29.760 --> 00:22:32.720
Charlie is not worried about locking&nbsp;
the door because he knows there’s no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.720 --> 00:22:35.120
threat. He and Jill are both in the room already.

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:39.600
Scre4m would be the final legacy of director&nbsp;
Wes Craven, but his creation would live on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:44.480
without him after yet another 11 year gap&nbsp;
with Scream (2022). I’ll be covering it&nbsp;&nbsp;

